Explanation: Paragraph 2 provides a detailed analysis of the experimental findings, including figures and results that directly contribute to understanding the main scientific contribution regarding the robustness of the network under adversary attacks. It also concludes with an important statement about the effectiveness of increasing the number of models in the debate against adversaries. On the other hand, Paragraph 1 sets up the context for evaluating the collaboration debate and discusses expectations versus actual outcomes when varying rounds or agents but does not delve into specific experimental results or conclusions as deeply as Paragraph 2.
[paragraph_channel_split] sample=1/5 attempt=1/8 temperature=0.026115557095103006
Paragraph 1: technical=95, citation=5
Paragraph econd": {
    "total_score": 0.026388956079621977,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "In this section, we evaluate the collaboration debate under different settings. We evaluate the effect of increasing the number of rounds or agents in the debate. While we would expect greater robustness with more agents or rounds, this is not always the case. In fact, increasing the number of rounds often has the opposite effect.",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  },
  "Paragraph2": {
    "total_score": 0.025354095056891703,
    "has_citations": false,
    "text": "Increasing the Number of Rounds . Figure 6 shows the Majority V ote Accuracy with 3 agents (N= 3) and an increasing number of rounds ( T= 1, . . . , 9) on TruthfulQA. We analyze whether agents can recover from the adversary attack with a higher number of rounds. We would like to see the models leveraging their own knowledge and reason- ing process to counteract the adversary. However, we observe this is not the case; once the models in the group change their answer to the wrong answer, they do not retract from it and the number of rounds is not an appropriate defence in this scenario. we analyze the network\u2019s robustness with a fixed number of rounds, T=3, and an increas- ing number of agents participating in the debate (M= 2, ...,6). The results of these experiments on TruthfulQA are shown in Figure 7. As generally expected, when M=2, the accuracy of the systemis close to 0, as there is only the adversary answer and one other model to generate an answer. As the number of agents increases, the overall system accuracy also improves. However, the accuracy over rounds drops in a similar proportion. This can prove the adversary is effectively persuading the agents in the network, regardless of the number of agents collaborating. Therefore, we conclude the increased number of models in the debate, provides better results generally but still under the effect of an adversary.",
    "citation_focus_text": ""
  }
}
